<p>
James Shore posits that each person-month spent writing code results in 10 days
of maintenance in the first year, then 5 days every year after that. Naïvely
interpreted, this is a <i>threshold</i>-based model for induced maintenance, and its
interesting because it&rsquo;s so simple.
</p>



<p>
If it could be shown to be true, that&rsquo;d be useful. The argument Shore is
making about the effects of robot-generated code does not depend on the specific
numbers, so Shore&rsquo;s numbers come from a hypothetical wisdom-of-the-crowds
aggregation rather than concrete data. But no hypotheticals are necessary here.
We have a thing called git.
</p>



<p>
Let&rsquo;s say we have a way of labeling each commit as either &ldquo;new development&rdquo; or
&ldquo;maintenance&rdquo;. Then we can count how many commits of each type there are in a
month. Possibly better, we can count how much
complexity has been handled in a month.
</p>



